# Line endings are LF in the working tree on every platform.
#
# This is PREVENTION, and it is labelled as prevention because the suite does
# not currently depend on it: the whole test suite passes in a genuine CRLF
# checkout, verified by cloning with core.autocrlf=true and running it there.
# Nothing in this repository compares a committed file to text it generates,
# which is the comparison that breaks.
#
# It is here because this repository is forked and run on other people's
# machines, so the cost of the gap is a stranger's first impression rather than
# one red build of ours. Two things measured on this repo rather than assumed:
#
#   - A Windows-style clone without this file checks out 79 of 95 tracked files
#     with CRLF. With it, none.
#   - Those CRLFs carry through the build into the generated HTML, so a fork
#     built on Windows produces output that differs byte for byte from the same
#     fork built on Linux. The pages render identically, but any future check
#     that compares built output to a committed expectation would fail for a
#     Windows forker and nobody else.
#
# The markdown and front-matter parsers accept CRLF on purpose (see the \r?\n
# handling in scripts/lib/fm.js and scripts/lib/md.js) and that stays true: a
# seller's own content is theirs to author however they like. This governs what
# lands in the repository, not what the engine will read.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Nothing here is binary text, but declare the shapes the store ships so an
# asset is never line-ending converted into a corrupt file.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
